{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And these are the ordinances.</b> One needs to know the reason for His saying <b>ve-eleh</b> (and these), and behold, they disagreed in the Mekhilta — Rabbi Yishmael and Rabbi Akiva. Rabbi Yishmael says: to tell you that just as the upper ones [commandments] were from Sinai, so too the lower ones were from Sinai. Rabbi Akiva says: ״וְאֵלֶּה״ — one might think they rehearse them but do not understand them, Scripture therefore says ״וְאֵלֶּה״ etc. — set them before them like a set table; thus far. Now according to Rabbi Yishmael, who explains ״וְאֵלֶּה״ as coming to say that they were stated at Sinai, it is necessarily the case that one must explain that the reason the verse needed to inform us of this is to teach about the specific details of this commandment that they were stated from Sinai — for if about their general principles, behold Rabbi Yishmael said in the last chapter of Zevachim (115b) that all the commandments were stated in their general principles at Sinai and their specifics at the Tent of Meeting; thus far — and if so, these ordinances too were included in the general principle, and it would not have been necessary to say ״וְאֵלֶּה״ to teach about this matter. And according to Rabbi Akiva, who disagreed with Rabbi Yishmael there in the baraita cited in Zevachim, that all the commandments were stated in their general principles and their specifics at Sinai, he cannot explain ״וְאֵלֶּה הַמִּשְׁפָּטִים״ as coming to teach that they were stated at Sinai, for he has no need to make us hear this, and it becomes necessary to explain it in another manner." "And Rashi's words require examination, for he explained here according to the opinion of Rabbi Yishmael, while in parashat Behar Sinai he explained according to Rabbi Akiva who said: just as the Sabbatical year was stated in its general principles and its specifics at Sinai, so too the entire Torah, etc. — and these are two divergent explanations. He should have explained here ״וְאֵלֶּה הַמִּשְׁפָּטִים״ as adding to the first matter what He commanded in a second matter — that one must set before them like a set table, whether ordinances of the lower realm or ordinances that preceded them." "<b>And what</b> I saw that the Rav, Rabbi Elijah Mizrachi, wrote — that the derashah of Rabbi Yishmael is that the ordinances were stated with voices and lightning — his words do not appear correct, nor are they acceptable, to say that HaShem spoke more than the Ten Statements. Even if Rabbi Yishmael were to explicitly say so, it would require force and proof for his words, all the more so since Rabbi Yishmael only said ״just as the upper ones were from Sinai״ and did not say ״just as the upper ones were with voices and lightning״ — and these things are not to be heeded. And if because of the difficulty we raised, we have already resolved it with a correct resolution: that the verse comes for the fine points of interpretation, as with the law of the Sabbatical year, about which the verse says at Mount Sinai. And Rabbi Yishmael is necessarily forced to concede regarding the Sabbatical year that its general principles and its specifics were stated at Sinai, as is explicit in the Torah, except that he did not hold to derive from it for the entire Torah. And perhaps his reason is that because the mention of Mount Sinai appears in connection with four commandments, he did not wish to derive from them for the entire Torah — but the main point is as we have written. And it is more correct to leave the matter as requiring examination of Rashi's words than to interpret them as he [Mizrachi] did, for he understood in Rabbi Yishmael's words that HaShem stated ״these ordinances״ in the same manner that He stated the Ten Statements." "And if the intent of the Rav, Rabbi Elijah Mizrachi, is to say that they were stated with voices alone and not in the order of the Ten Statements, this too is not so, for this would constitute a third structure in the order of divine speech: the first is the order in which HaShem spoke the Ten Statements; the second, the order that was habitual for speaking with him [Moses] all forty days, [communicating] all the commandments of the Torah; the third, the order of the utterance of ״these ordinances.״ And who would incline his ear to hear such things, to construct a reality for which we find no parallel in the Torah? And furthermore one could raise an objection, except that the matter is self-evident." "Further, it appears possible to say that the reason for His saying <b>ve-eleh</b> alludes to their words — may their memory be for blessing (Sanhedrin 86b) — that one must seat judges before the Sanctuary; therefore He said <b>ve-eleh</b>, adding to the first matter that He mentioned nearby — the place where the Sanctuary dwells — for all are in one place." "<b>Which you shall set before them.</b> The reason for His speaking in this language — even though our Sages of blessed memory derived homilies from it (Sanhedrin 7a) — requires knowing the plain sense of the matter. And perhaps, since there are portions of the Torah that constitute a personal obligation upon every individual to know what he must practice, and without which they are not parties to the covenant of the Torah — the parable for this is: if one does not know that it is forbidden to eat trefah, then he eats it, and likewise blood and forbidden fat; and in positive commandments — Pesah, matzah and maror, cessation on consecrated days — these one is obligated to know according to their [the Sages'] opinion — yet there is another dimension, such as the laws of plaintiff and defendant, the laws of sanctifying the new moon, the laws of sacrifices and the like. The truth is that knowledge of the Torah is an obligation upon every Israelite, but it will not detract in the least from a righteous person if he lacks knowledge of them, as long as there are great men in Israel who judge and sanctify and instruct laws for Israel. And HaShem was wise to say here <b>which you shall set before them</b>, to say that these laws must be heard by every Israelite, for without this, the buyer would not know at the time of purchase what he is acquiring, and also the one acquired does not know that HaShem freed him after six years to be claimed by the one who demands [freedom], and also the judges would not know of every buyer of a slave to inform him that he is obligated to send him [free]. Therefore He said <b>which you shall set before them</b>, for this law is among the laws that must be set before all of them. And therefore you will find that Scripture speaks in direct address — <b>When you acquire</b>, etc. — for it makes the matter known to every buyer." "<b>Further,</b> it intends to explain the commandments that are received by all, and therefore it says <b>which you shall set before them</b> — meaning: although this matter involves a deficiency of will, for the one who acquires a slave would hasten to send him free after six years, you shall set the matter before them because this commandment is for the merit of every one of them — when he is a slave he will not serve forever, and through this he will be reconciled to the matter, for every one of them will appreciate the thing as being for his own benefit, since every Hebrew who is sold is so only because of his distress, or he stole and the court sold him, and this is brought about by poverty, whereas at [Sinai] all of them were wealthy — so this is only for something that turns in the cycle, and each one shall set before himself that the kindness applies to him, lest the wheel turn upon him, and this is a merit for him."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The Mekhilta citations are rendered in their standard form; the abbreviation וְגוֹ׳ is expanded as 'etc.' throughout.", 2 "The divine name ה׳ is consistently rendered as HaShem per voice rules."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:31:45.505748084Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}